[=] Generate the charter map from charter metadata $NEEDS DECISION: should a charter's frontmatter description become a real model field (today it is invisible to the tool and silently dropped by every CLI charter write)? Until that is settled, the charter map has no metadata to generate from. Full analysis below.charters README and someday README are hand-maintained tables of contents and drifted; derive membership, state and description from frontmatter, keep priority order as a human judgment. DEFERRED 2026-09-18: this is a clearhead-core feature (a new generation command/library function), out of scope for the platform+specifications-only overnight pass. Not started.STOPPED 2026-09-18 (overnight clearhead-core run, task 6/7) — the task's own premise does not hold in the codebase yet, and making it hold is a charter-data-model decision, not generation work. No code was written and neither README was touched.WHAT IS ALREADY AVAILABLE: membership and state are derivable today. The loaded model gives each charter's `state` and its `parent` (declared or inferred from the directory: `someday/graph-federation/README.md` → parent `someday`), so "which charters belong to this map" and "what state is each in" need no new data. Priority ORDER stays human: the existing entries' order and numbering can be preserved while their metadata is refreshed.WHAT BLOCKS IT — a charter has no short description in metadata. `CharterFrontmatter` (clearhead-core/src/workspace/charter.rs) has exactly id/title/alias/parent/objectives/state and NO `description`; `Charter.description` is parsed from the document BODY (`extract_title_and_description`), and `format_charter` writes it back into the body. So the frontmatter `description:` lines that agent-surface and six `someday/` bets DO carry are invisible to the tool, and cannot be "derived from frontmatter" by any generator: there is nothing to read.REPRODUCED CONSEQUENCE (pre-existing, not caused by tonight's work): because that field is unmodeled, any CLI charter write drops it. In a scratch workspace, a charter whose README.md declared `description: A short summary ...` and whose body was one prose line lost the frontmatter line entirely after `clearhead update charter <it> --state active` — the model's description stayed the body text and `format_charter` emits only id/alias/parent/objectives/state plus the body. The 8 documents in this workspace that carry the field (agent-surface + the six `someday/` bets + `someday/README.md`) are therefore silently rewritten by `update charter` / `close charter` / `jot`.THE UNDECIDED CALLS (why I stopped rather than guessing):1. Is the frontmatter `description:` the authoritative short description, with the body kept as longer prose? If so, `Charter`/`MarkdownCharter`/`CharterFrontmatter` need the field, `parse_charter` must prefer it, and `format_charter` must emit it — otherwise the silent drop above stays. If instead the body stays authoritative, then the someday map's summaries have no metadata home and the entry text must keep being hand-written, which is the thing this action exists to remove.2. What exactly regenerates? The root map's per-entry text is priority RATIONALE ("CLI friction surfaced by real use: query output consistency, charter actions-file creation, ..."), which exists nowhere else and is not a charter description; several charters it lists (support, pure-core-split, deployment) have no description field at all. The `someday/` map is the clean case — every entry is a charter whose frontmatter description is a one-sentence summary that closely matches the hand-written text. So "generate the map" means different things in the two documents.3. The generated form is unspecified: alias only, alias + state, or alias + state + description; numbered like the root map or bulleted like the someday map; whether unknown members are appended or merely reported; whether the root map's non-charter entry ("Objective integration") and its prose sections (Settled prerequisites, Housekeeping notes) are preserved verbatim or must be excluded from the regenerated region.SUGGESTED NEXT STEP: settle (1) first — make `description` a real frontmatter field on the charter model and fix the silent drop (that is a bounded, valuable change on its own, and it would also fix the same class of loss for any other unmodeled key), then the generator becomes mechanical: for the owner charter's direct children, keep the existing entry order, refresh ` [[alias]] ` + state + description, report (or append) children the list omits. Until then, this action stays blocked.$ ^2026-09-14T17:09 #01a0a266-01c4-75e3-a35c-ed455d2e2f5f
[ ] Let actions wait on calendar events =event-triggers $The human's insight, 2026-10-02 (open enrollment): when an action becomes possible is a third thing, distinct from when we intend to start it (@) and when it is due (:). It is often an event in the world, already on a calendar. So < (waits on) may point at a calendar event as well as an action, and the event's time feeds the action's derived notBefore (Decision 47). Rejected with the human: a third date sigil (another character banned from titles; copies a shared date into every gated action; loses the event's identity). Calendar sync is unchanged: only @ with | places a block; : and < are constraints, and the triggering event is already on its calendar. Hard part: the workspace must see other calendars. Sketch: read-only vdir sources in configuration (shared across workspaces, unlike plans/ which one workspace owns and writes), synced by existing tools such as vdirsyncer, never written by ClearHead; events resolve by UID or by SUMMARY, ambiguity a lint warning as for action names; only referenced events enter the graph (size, privacy). A trigger is a moment, so 'once it begins' and 'once it is over' coincide; < on an event with duration, and recurring events (which occurrence?), wait for a real case. Keep app-vocabulary open to it: a wait targets an action or an event. 2026-10-02 with the human: the CalDAV server also hosts every workspace's plans collections, so a read-only mirror of the whole account would show ClearHead plans as events. Recommended: a source names one collection (one calendar's vdir), never an account root; which calendars count as the world is chosen in the vdirsyncer pair (collections = \[...]), an existing tool. ClearHead plans carry no reliable marker today (X-CLEARHEAD-STATUS only when blocked; no PRODID), so content cannot tell a plan from an event; if a guard is wanted, stamp plans with an X-CLEARHEAD property and have doctor warn when a source contains one. Waiting on another workspace's plan means waiting on its action being done, not on a time passing: that is a cross-workspace action dependency, a separate feature, never smuggled in through calendars. SETTLED 2026-10-02 (human): sources are chosen calendars, e.g. the core personal calendar, or a designated integration calendar when it gets more technical; selection in vdirsyncer. CORRECTED 2026-10-02 (human: do not depend on vdirsyncer; the app stays ignorant of transport): calendar selection lives in ClearHead's configuration, a list of local paths, one calendar each, not in any sync tool's config. A source is a vdir directory or a single multi-component .ics file (the export nearly every calendar service offers), so anyone can fill it: vdirsyncer, Syncthing, a cron'd download, or by hand. ClearHead never fetches a URL. Earlier mentions of selection in vdirsyncer above are superseded. Identity (human, 2026-10-02): rely on the RFC 5545 UID as the event's persistent identity, as every calendar client must. The same UID in two sources (an invitation copied into two calendars) is one event; lint only if the copies disagree on time.$ !2 ^2026-10-02T14:41 #01a0fe90-77f8-71a6-98df-c7a7faca3693
[ ] Print the charter alias, not the UUID, in activation hints $doctor.rs:249, action.rs:1368 and init.rs:133 print 'clearhead update charter <uuid> --state active'. The resolver already takes aliases, so print the alias when the charter has one and fall back to the UUID. Check first that an alias colliding across additional workspaces fails loudly rather than resolving to the wrong charter.$ ^2026-10-07T21:21 #01a119be-fdf5-7114-bace-b52fbbde6e2c
[-] Move Habits into habits files (Decision 57) =core-habit ~ $After spec-habits releases and Core bumps its pin (Decision 54). Split into small reviewed steps before dispatch: Habits, the calendar, sync and templates meet here. Read Habits from <charter>.habits.actions; add each arrived slot's occurrence to <charter>.actions (ids from (Habit, slot), steps from (Habit, slot, step)) whether or not earlier ones are done; stamp the template (children win over a template line); project each Habit to a recurring VEVENT in plans/ and merge calendar edits back (series to the line, overrides to the occurrence); keep calendar-only facts in sidecars. Every *.actions reader learns the suffix in the same cycle (Core's assembly.rs file discovery, the nvim plugin's init.lua). Kind-change detection from the merge base with accept, revert, defer and --accept-all; recurring to one-off keeps the newest open occurrence's id. Clean-break migration: rewrite existing vdir Habits into habits files once, without carrying old event-derived ids. Clear due_recurrence and the VTODO comments in crates/clearhead-core/src/domain/mod.rs around Plan. Implement Decision 57 amendments de574ba, f35c4fb, 2c2d28a and 19520b4 after the specification releases: retain native UUID on text moves and accepted calendar one-off to recurring conversion; occurrences retain distinct derived ids. Require user-confirmed linking after transport UID replacement. Sync presents links at arrival; ambiguous relationships defer and recur next sync; suspected replacements with no candidate remain held until manually linked, with no new entity created. Doctor diagnoses held relationships read-only; --fix delegates to the same explicit link operation as sync, never merges native identities. Test these paths including DAVx5-style UID replacement and series splits, independently of same-UID proposals. Split work before dispatch into reviewed increments with executable acceptance: graph/schema/mapping/fixture conformance; cross-device date-only, floating, zoned and DST slot identity (coordinate slot-key-zone); stable step ids across repeated stamping; resumable bounded catch-up preserving every missed slot; duplicate suppression against active and archived occurrences; rule edits preserving existing history; and an exhaustive reader/discovery/archive inventory, including nvim, with tests and coordinated pin/release updates. Escalate unresolved product policy rather than inventing matching thresholds, backlog loss or identity reconciliation. Planning split committed 2026-10-26: child chain core-habit-pins through core-habit-ship; each increment has executable acceptance and independent review before landing. Implementation starts only after published release-habits adoption. REVIEW FINDINGS 1-4 IMPLEMENTATION HANDOFF (2026-11-03; mechanics removed from the specification, not abandoned). Collection loading attaches by exact canonical collection ownership even without a directory, never alias/title/next basename; quarantine unowned collections as unowned-plans-collection, never create implicit charters. Doctor may explicitly remove them after warning that transport can propagate deletion. VEVENT transition: retired plan_component/legacy vtodo warns; for one transition release atomically convert linked VTODO masters and all same-UID overrides, preserving UID, frames, alarms, unknown properties and paths wherever representable. Unsupported/lossy conversion leaves the resource unchanged and reports why; unlinked VTODOs are not adopted and doctor reports them. One-way export plans may still write VTODO without readback. Store native/transport links, RDATE/EXDATE, frames and pending overrides in committed Habit/Action/charter semantic sidecars, never only in the reconstructible machine-local projection store. Store merge bases there; losing it must not lose identity or recurrence-only facts. Reconcile name, human description (excluding leading directives), planned start/end, recurrence and exceptions by independent field-wise three-way merge bases; safe fields proceed despite another conflict, first sync presents differing values rather than timestamp winners. Determine deletion of an established resource from the merge base, not an untracked snapshot. Detect kind changes against that base; offer accept/revert (restore last synchronized kind)/defer (ask again next sync). Scripted --accept-all accepts kind changes only; other noninteractive runs defer, and neither substitutes for explicit identity-link confirmation. At UID replacement or series split arrival, sync presents explicit linking; ambiguous relationships defer and recur next sync; no-candidate suspected replacements remain held without adoption until manually linked. Doctor diagnoses holds read-only; --fix delegates to the same explicit link operation, never merges native identities. Catch-up runs on write-path tick/sync, never reads: bounded per transaction, resumable after durable writes, interruption-safe, reports remaining backlog, scans active and archived identities before stamping, and never uses a mutable last-open token. Persist template-step UUIDs before stamping. Rule edits re-evaluate arrived slots against both stores; old checkpoints cannot hide newly introduced slots. Clean-break migration rewrites existing vdir-owned prescriptions once into habits files, carries no old event-derived occurrence ids, is explicit/previewable/restart-safe, and leaves no dual source. Published specification and grammar releases precede Core adoption; no release or pin movement in this review. Finding 4 amended: all open Habit states generate (including blocked); completed and cancelled stop all generation including catch-up, without closing occurrences. No pause capability: close and recreate. The review finding writes \[-] for cancelled, but existing syntax is \[_]; preserve existing symbols pending the linked human clarification. Storage clarification: merge bases belong to the machine-local reconstructible projection store; native links and recurrence-only facts belong to committed semantic sidecars. Human clarifications are tracked as =habit-cancel-marker (symbol wording) and =habit-unrecur-no-open (existing unresolved kind-change edge case). AUTONOMOUS CONTINUATION GO (facilitator, 2026-11-04): after corrected 0.11.1 pin adoption, continue this Habit child chain one independently reviewed cycle at a time while pace allows; worker/reviewer different vendors, route by current pace, four steward predictions per dispatch. Stop at anything requiring Darrion, file +human NEEDS DECISION and keep work preserved. A large spend is raised with facilitator first. Every breakpoint must have current actions and committed/pushed work.$ !2 ^2026-10-11T17:51 <spec-habits #01a12c17-0314-7f10-8628-3f27adcb36ac
    >[x] Implement native Habit graph and schema conformance =core-habit-graph $One small reviewed increment after completed core-habit-pins. Published specification v0.5.0 at 69b6781 and registry grammar 0.11.1 are now pinned; use those contracts, never change the specification to fit code. Add named habit_graph_conformance tests first, run them red (prove nonzero tests), then implement native Habit identity/state and required rule in domain/graph/schema projection; clean obsolete due_recurrence and VTODO comments around domain::Plan where compatible with this increment. Match action/sidecar JSON, app vocabulary/SHACL and CCO mapping. Acceptance: project released Habit fixture values through real Core domain/graph machinery (not hand-forged RDF assertions); check exact expected triples, NotStarted/open and Completed/Cancelled with closed-date semantics, required rule, canonical STRING slot values, native Habit lineage and no template child Action leakage; existing graph/schema suites stay green. CORRECTION to the 2026-10-26 brief: Active was stale; Darrion finding 5 and approved v0.5.0 explicitly use app:NotStarted for \[ ] and the Action state vocabulary for Habits. Reader/discovery/archive work is core-habit-readers, canonical slot generation is core-habit-slots, and catch-up/calendar/migration follow later; do not pull those workstreams into this graph/domain increment. Do not redesign mutable transaction APIs while occurrence-model-owner remains undecided; if this increment truly requires that decision, stop and file +human NEEDS DECISION with evidence. Complete Core scripts/gate.sh, cargo test --locked, affected spec-conformance and full platform scripts/validate-pinned, doctor no new violations/warnings before landing. Worker commits without pushing; independent reviewer from another vendor checks spec coherence and approach before land. No zero-test passes, fixture copies, source policy changes, local registry patches or weakened tests/gates. Record new dependencies explicitly, prefer small reuse and pure Core boundary. Implemented in Core 18e8f91: native Habit definitions (Action lifecycle vocabulary, required rule and recurrence anchor, terminal-only closed dates), separate template children, canonical Habit JSON export, native occurrence sidecar facts and graph occurrenceOf/STRING slot projection independent of transport identifiers. Shared planned-block projection; legacy Plan due_recurrence retained for compatibility with corrected VEVENT/legacy comments. No readers, slot generation, catch-up, calendar mutation, migration, specification changes or transaction API redesign. Tests-first evidence: five named habit_graph_conformance tests executed and failed on missing behavior before implementation; seven now pass, including exact released fixture triples plus required derived plannedFrom, all states/closed-date rejection, rule requirement, template isolation, native D/F/U and fractional STRING slots, action/sidecar JSON schema validation, and Core-produced RDF through published SHACL and CCO mappings. Validation: complete Core scripts/gate.sh, cargo test --locked, full workspace spec-conformance (including 7 Habit and 2 existing app graph tests), and full platform scripts/validate-pinned all green. Doctor remains 0 violations/13 existing warnings. No new dependencies: reused existing Core crates and platform uv/pyshacl toolchain. Independent other-vendor review remains required before landing; worker did not push.$ !2 ^2026-10-26T05:15 %2026-11-04T07:38 <core-habit-pins #01a176a3-0d30-72d0-a5a2-9813d611c428
