[x] Re-brief \=core-habit-steps as three runs, after chst1 stopped =chst-rebrief $Handoff from the facilitator. Darrion 2026-11-07 chose stop and re-brief at =chst1-decide: six rounds did not converge (the written-id refusal open since review /2 through two fix rounds) and findings surfaced one at a time, the increment-too-big shape (=review-shape answer: a structural cause is a re-brief, not a patch). Run chst1 is stopped, nothing landed; its workspace (agent run 20261105-133227 and later) is evidence to build from. Split, in order, each its own reviewed run: (1) the named-template reader (read_template_actions via parse_actions) keeps the parser's is_id_generated record, so stamping can refuse steps without a written #uuid from data, not a caller flag; (2) stamping as one remap from template to stamped steps: ids, predecessor links, plan_id and external_occurrence_key together, plus the self-dependency lint and stamping error from =habit-self-dependency-spec once Darrion approves it; facilitator's unverified guess is that copy-then-patch is the shared cause, so check it in the design; (3) child times in a DST gap follow canonical_slot's before-gap rule (LA 2026-03-08 02:30 is 10:30Z). You draft the briefs; the 2026-11-05 NativeOccurrence.key note in the charter log still applies after (2).  UPDATE 2026-11-07: the self-dependency lint in step (2) is not approved (Darrion meant templates carrying R:, see =template-recurrence-spec); stamping refusing a template with recurrence belongs in (2) instead.  UPDATE 2026-11-07 later: Darrion approved refusing self-dependency as part of a general rule (no Action depends on an ancestor), see =template-recurrence-spec; step (2) refuses both a template carrying R: and a step depending on its Habit, once that spec lands.  DONE 2026-11-07 (steward): split filed as children of =core-habit-steps: =chst-reader (needs no spec change), =chst-stamp (after =chst-reader and Darrion's =template-recurrence-approve), =chst-dst (after =chst-stamp); the general lint is =lint-ancestor-dependency.$ !2 +steward ^2026-11-07T07:30 %2026-11-07T18:01 #01a1b4ea-8b5e-75b3-a43c-680c18bd6725
[x] Re-draft the template-recurrence rule from Darrion's real meaning; hold E009 self-dependency =template-recurrence-spec $Darrion 2026-11-07 at =habit-self-dependency-approve: 'i wasnt even thinking about this edge case i was thinking about a template that itself had recurrence ... that is a filter to make sure templates dont use recurrence.' So the 11-05 quote was about R: inside a template, not a step depending on its Habit; the draft on specifications draft/habit-self-dependency (5d88097) misread it. What the spec already says (linting.md, checked by the facilitator): E002 already makes R: on a Habit's inline child a violation; R: in a named template is only a semantic warning (Habit scope and template warnings), and process.md (Habits) does not say stamping refuses either. Darrion wants both a lint error and an error on generation, for inline and named templates; ordinary non-template .actions keep the warning. Self-dependency (the old E009) is a separate edge case Darrion has not ruled on: keep it off this branch until they do. When you ask Darrion to approve, put the GitHub compare link in the action (https://github.com/ClearHeadToDo-Devs/specifications/compare/main...<branch>); Darrion 2026-11-07 could not find the last draft from its branch name.  ANSWERED 2026-11-07, self-dependency: Darrion: 'yes i think thats a bug because we cant be pointed at a habit right? ... subactions shouldnt depend on parents anyways they are already related by virtue of being a subchild, the dependency is implicit.' So the rule is general, not Habit-only: an Action may not depend (<) on any of its ancestors; lint error, and stamping refuses a template step that does. Facilitator's supporting reading of the spec: process.md (On Child Actions) calls a terminal parent with open descendants contradictory, so a child waiting on its parent waits on something that waits on it, the W007 cycle in another form. Steps within one occurrence may still depend on sibling steps. Put both rules on one branch for Darrion's approval, with the compare link.  DONE 2026-11-07 (steward): draft on specifications draft/template-recurrence (139f0c9), both rules: E002 covers named templates, E009 any ancestor dependency; approval at =template-recurrence-approve with the compare link. Old draft branch deleted.$ !2 +steward ^2026-11-07T08:17 %2026-11-07T18:01 #01a1b515-4d30-7d10-b2ab-2c2071f11238
[_] Draft a specification proposal for approvals as their own record =approval-record $Darrion's idea, 2026-11-07, handed over by the facilitator; you draft, Darrion approves the spec change. Darrion: 'approvals are a specific thing. in servicenow they are a dedicated record that can reference any type of record and is its own thing that i can review ... data with state that can just have a generic link so i can approve or deny actions, docs, whatever.' Why now: Darrion could not find the E009 draft, named only by branch inside an action; today approvals hide in at least four places (+human NEEDS DECISION actions, assistant discussions/ pages, review-cycle's decide step, spec draft branches). Facilitator's view, for you to test: a distinct record, not an action state, because a verdict (approved, rejected) is not an action lifecycle (rejected is not cancelled) and one approval references its subject rather than containing it. Open: the states (requested, approved, rejected, withdrawn?); the reference as one URI (urn:uuid for ClearHead items, a URL for anything else: compare link, doc, PR); a reason with each verdict, author and time; where it is stored (relation to =action-notes and the charter JSON sidecar); how a client lists pending ones and records a verdict; whether a verdict can trigger the waiting thing (e.g. review-cycle decide).  UPDATE 2026-11-07, Darrion: 'for us right now that is where our verdicts concept is built from but maybe is more generic? idk.' Compare with agent-sandbox verdicts (docs/DECISIONS.md, Decisions 2-4 and 2026-11-01): already the same shape, a judge, agree or disagree, a note, per-finding comments keyed by id, append-only, a human pending list, a feedback list and a hook that wakes a reader. Differences: a verdict's subject is always a landed commit, stored as git notes so it travels with the repo, and agent-sandbox deliberately works without ClearHead; and a verdict comes after the landing while an approval gates something. Facilitator's view, a guess to test: gate versus feedback belongs to whatever waits (review-cycle decide waiting on an approval makes it a gate), not to the record, so a verdict is an approval nobody waits on. Then the proposal defines the record's shape so a landing verdict fits it, without moving verdicts' storage out of git or making agent-sandbox depend on ClearHead; one pending view reads both.  STARTING SHAPE 2026-11-07, agreed with Darrion in the facilitator's session ('youre mostly right here of having it be a log'): two append-only record kinds; state derived, never stored. request: id, subject (one URI naming an exact version, e.g. a compare link to a commit, a ClearHead urn, a doc, a landed commit's review), question (one line), context (link to its page), asked_of, asked_by, at, optional follows (the earlier request it continues). response: request, stance approve|reject|comment, optional on (a part of the subject: finding id, section), note, by, at. A request is open until its asked_of approves or rejects, or its asker withdraws it. No revise stance (Darrion: not sure it is needed, or it is a different kind of response): a reject with a note sends work back, and the waiter decides whether the same steward looks again or a new run starts; the new request names the old one with follows, so the thread stays one conversation. Gate-ness lives in the waiter (an action or decide step points at the request). Verdicts fit as approvals whose subject is a review, stored in git notes as now. Left out: multiple approvers and chains. Open, yours: where it is stored.  AMENDED 2026-11-07, Darrion: 'either responses or even requests can have an optional predecesor (just like actions!) so that if there is a "conversation" we can easily construct that history.' So follows becomes predecessor, optional on both record kinds, pointing at any approval record (a request continuing an earlier one, a response answering a response). Facilitator's note for the spec: an action's predecessor blocks its successor; here it only orders a conversation, so say which relation it maps to, so graph queries over predecessors do not read a reply as a blocker.  CORRECTED 2026-11-07, Darrion: 'no approval are not part of clearhead i never said it needed to be ... it can reference clearhead records but we have ids. we are taking a note from databases and integrating at a data level not a type or deps level.' The facilitator filed this here by analogy with =action-notes, unasked. Approvals are their own data, referencing ClearHead (and anything else) by id; not a ClearHead spec change. Steward: do not draft this. Held for the facilitator until Darrion names the owner, then it moves there.  MOVED 2026-11-07 to meta-analysis =approval-record (Darrion's choice).$ !2 +facilitator ^2026-11-07T08:40 %2026-11-07T09:35 #01a1b52a-621d-78f0-84d7-a016ab38b004
